GLOSSARY_EXTRA = [{'term': 'Slip', 'def': 'The difference between synchronous speed Ns and actual rotor speed Nr in an induction motor: slip = (Ns minus Nr) divided by Ns. Typical values: 2 to 4 percent at rated load, near 0 percent at no load, 100 percent at stall. Slip is the mechanism that generates torque: relative motion between rotor conductors and the rotating magnetic field induces rotor current by transformer action, and that current in the field produces force. Higher load demand means more torque needed, more slip, more induced rotor current, and more torque up to the breakdown torque limit. Excessive slip above 5 to 6 percent at rated load with no added mechanical load indicates high rotor-bar resistance from a degraded or cracked rotor.'}, {'term': 'Synchronous Speed', 'def': 'The speed of the rotating magnetic field in an AC induction motor stator: Ns = 120 x f / P, where f is line frequency in Hz and P is the number of poles. For a 4-pole motor at 60 Hz, Ns = 1800 RPM. Actual rotor speed is slightly less due to slip of 2 to 4 percent at rated load, so nameplate reads approximately 1750 RPM. Synchronous speed scales with frequency: a VFD at 90 Hz raises Ns to 2700 RPM. More poles mean slower speed: 6-pole equals 1200 RPM, 8-pole equals 900 RPM, 12-pole equals 600 RPM. The motor cannot reach Ns because slip is the mechanism that induces rotor current and produces torque.'}, {'term': 'RMS', 'def': 'Root Mean Square, the equivalent DC value that delivers the same average power to a resistive load as an AC waveform. For a pure sinusoid: V_RMS = V_peak divided by 1.414. The 480 V nameplate voltage is RMS; actual peak is 480 x 1.414 = 679 V. Averaging RMS meters assume a pure sine wave and read correctly only for sinusoidal signals. True-RMS meters calculate actual RMS of any waveform by squaring instantaneous values, averaging, then taking the square root — required for VFD outputs, non-sinusoidal drive currents, and any distorted power signal. An averaging meter on a VFD-distorted waveform typically reads 5 to 30 percent low.'}, {'term': 'Power Factor', 'def': 'The ratio of real power in kW doing useful work to apparent power in kVA representing total current times voltage: PF = P/S = cosine of theta, where theta is the phase angle between voltage and current. PF of 1.0 means all current does useful work; PF of 0.7 means 30 percent is reactive current magnetizing inductive loads. Low PF increases line current for the same real work, causing excess I-squared-R heating and voltage drop. Many utilities penalize industrial customers for PF below 0.90 to 0.95 via reactive demand charges. Correction: add power factor correction capacitors that inject leading reactive current to cancel the lagging reactive current of inductive motor loads.'}, {'term': 'KVL', 'def': 'Kirchhoff Voltage Law: the sum of all voltage rises and drops around any closed loop equals zero. In a 24 VDC series circuit with a relay coil at 20 V and a suppression diode at 4 V, KVL confirms 20+4=24 V. Troubleshooting: measure voltage drop across each series element and sum them. If the sum does not equal source voltage, a hidden resistance exists such as a corroded terminal, loose connection, or high-resistance contact adding an unaccounted drop. The element showing an unexpectedly high voltage drop is the fault. KVL is the basis for half-split voltage diagnostics on series control circuits.'}, {'term': 'KCL', 'def': 'Kirchhoff Current Law: the algebraic sum of all currents at any node equals zero — what flows in must flow out. ΣI_in = ΣI_out. If a 24 VDC bus feeds three parallel relay coils, supply current equals the sum of all three coil currents. An unexpected imbalance between supply current and the sum of measured load currents reveals a sneak path, leakage current, or short circuit drawing unreported current. KCL also explains how current divides in parallel branches in proportion to conductance, where lower resistance carries more current.'}, {'term': "Ohm's Law", 'def': 'V = I x R, the fundamental relationship between voltage in volts, current in amperes, and resistance in ohms. Rearranged: I = V/R finds current; R = V/I finds resistance. With P = V x I, power formulas become P = I-squared x R for heat from current through resistance and P = V-squared / R. Troubleshooting: a 24 VDC relay coil rated 500 ohm should draw 24/500=48 mA; measuring 10 mA means resistance rose due to corrosion or supply voltage dropped. Ohm Law explains wire voltage drop: each ohm of loop resistance drops I x R volts that the load does not receive, which is why wire sizing matters on long 24 VDC runs to remote sensors.'}, {'term': 'Live Zero', 'def': 'The use of 4 mA as the zero-scale minimum in a 4-20 mA current loop so that 0 mA unmistakably signals a fault condition rather than a process-at-zero reading. A healthy transmitter at its minimum measurable value outputs 4 mA. If the receiver sees 0 mA, it knows the wire is broken, the transmitter has lost power, or a connector has failed. A 0-10 V signal cannot distinguish process at zero from a broken wire since both read 0 V. Live-zero loops automatically detect open circuits, failed transmitters, and cable damage without additional hardware. The 4 mA convention is universal in process instrumentation and HART-protocol transmitters.'}, {'term': 'Quadrature', 'def': 'Two encoder channels A and B generating square waves 90 degrees out of phase. Phase lead reveals direction: A leads B on forward rotation, B leads A on reverse. The X4 decoder counts all four signal transitions per encoder line for 4x resolution: a 1000-line encoder gives 4000 counts per revolution. The Z index channel produces one pulse per revolution for homing and position verification. Quadrature is standard on virtually all industrial servo and stepper encoder feedback because it provides both direction and speed without a separate signal. Noise on one channel causes count errors; always route encoder cables in separate conduit from power wiring.'}, {'term': 'Absolute Encoder', 'def': 'An encoder that outputs a unique binary code word representing exact shaft angle at all times, so position is known at power-up with no homing required. Multi-turn absolute encoders track multiple revolutions using a gear train or battery-backed counter. Used in servo systems where re-homing is unacceptable: robot arms, vertical axes, filling heads, index tables. Absolute encoders cost more than incremental types. Single-turn absolute repeats every 360 degrees; multi-turn tracks cumulative position across power cycles. Common interfaces include HIPERFACE, EnDat, and BISS-C serial protocols.'}, {'term': 'Incremental Encoder', 'def': 'Generates A and B pulse trains as the shaft rotates, 90 degrees out of phase for quadrature direction sensing. A leads B for forward rotation, B leads A for reverse. Position is known only relative to last home; power loss loses the count and re-homing is required at startup. X4 decoding counts all four signal transitions per encoder line for 4x resolution: a 1024-line encoder gives 4096 counts per revolution. The Z index channel gives one pulse per revolution for homing and position verification. Incremental encoders are standard in conveyor, spindle, and axis applications where startup homing is practical.'}, {'term': 'Input Image Table', 'def': 'A dedicated PLC memory buffer storing a snapshot of every physical input state, captured once at the start of each scan cycle. The PLC program uses this frozen image throughout the entire execution phase and never reads live input wires mid-scan. At scan end the output image table is written to physical outputs simultaneously. This scan-synchronous model ensures consistent data across all rungs but means the program is always one full scan behind physical reality. A pulse shorter than one scan time may not be captured. High-speed I/O such as encoder signals bypass the image table using interrupt-driven HSC modules that latch independently of scan timing.'}, {'term': 'Last-State-Wins', 'def': 'The PLC scan-order rule where writing the same output bit on multiple rungs or Structured Text assignments causes only the last-executed write to determine the final output value. A double-coil example: Rung 10 sets Motor_Run=TRUE; Rung 45 unconditionally sets Motor_Run=FALSE — Motor_Run is always FALSE regardless of Rung 10 logic. Studio 5000 generates a duplicate-output compiler warning but allows download. Detection: right-click the tag, choose Cross Reference, list all OTE/OTL/OTU/MOV write locations. Fix: consolidate all enabling conditions into one output rung, or use intentional OTL/OTU set-reset pairs where latching is truly needed.'}, {'term': 'Sinking', 'def': 'An NPN output or sensor that pulls its output wire to 0 V when active, sinking current from the load through its transistor to the return rail. The load connects between positive supply and the output wire; current flows from +V through the load into the NPN output to 0 V. Sinking sensors are traditional in US and Japanese equipment. Match to a current-sinking input card. Mixing a PNP sourcing sensor to a sinking input card results in an always-on or inverted input. Always verify sensor output type against input card type before wiring. Standard 3-wire NPN: brown = +24 V, blue = 0 V, black = signal output.'}, {'term': 'Sourcing', 'def': 'A PNP output or sensor that switches the positive supply rail to the output wire when active, sourcing current from the positive supply into the load and returning to 0 V. The load connects between the output wire and 0 V return. Sourcing sensors are standard in European-designed equipment. Match to a current-sourcing PNP-compatible input card. PNP outputs are safer in contaminated environments: the output wire is at +24 V when inactive, so a wire-to-ground fault draws current but does not create a spurious active signal. Standard 3-wire NPN wiring: brown = +24 V, blue = 0 V, black = signal.'}, {'term': 'OTL/OTU', 'def': 'Output Latch and Output Unlatch are retentive coil instructions: OTL sets a bit to TRUE and holds it even when the rung goes FALSE; OTU clears the same bit. The bit retains state through scan cycles and program edits. Common use: fault latch where OTL fires on detection and OTU fires on operator acknowledge. Critical rule: never mix OTL/OTU on a bit with an OTE coil on the same bit — OTE overwrites the latched state every scan via last-state-wins. Never use OTL on the same bit in multiple routines without careful sequencing. Use cross-reference to audit all writers of any latched bit.'}, {'term': 'TOF', 'def': 'Timer Off-Delay: the rung going TRUE sets the DN output bit immediately; when the rung goes FALSE the timer begins accumulating and DN stays TRUE until ACC reaches PRE then clears. Uses: cooling fan running 60 seconds after motor stops, warning light staying on 30 s after deviation clears, seal-bar heater holding temperature after jaw opens. Critical exam point: TOF DN is TRUE during timing, holding the output ON. It turns OFF at the end of the preset, the opposite of TON which turns ON at end of preset. Common mistake: wiring TOF where TON was intended, causing instant output with delayed turn-off.'}, {'term': 'RTO', 'def': 'Retentive Timer On-delay that accumulates elapsed time while the rung is TRUE and retains the ACC value when the rung goes FALSE — unlike TON which resets ACC immediately on rung drop. ACC only resets when an explicit RES instruction executes. Uses: cumulative motor runtime hours for oil-change scheduling, total run hours for warranty tracking, batch total-cycle-time accumulation across multiple production runs. In Studio 5000 the TONR function block in ST provides retentive behavior. Always document the reset trigger clearly — accidental resets cause incorrect maintenance intervals.'}, {'term': 'CTD', 'def': 'Count-Down counter that decrements its ACC value by 1 on each FALSE-to-TRUE transition of the CD input rung. When ACC reaches zero, the DN bit sets. Used in batch applications where you preload the preset count and count down to empty. Pairs with CTU for net up/down counting on the same accumulator. Reset via a RES instruction reloads ACC to the preset value. In Studio 5000, CTU and CTD can share an accumulator for inventory or net-displacement tracking. Always initialize CTD ACC to the desired starting count on first run — an uninitialized ACC of zero immediately sets DN on the first CD pulse.'}, {'term': 'State Machine', 'def': 'A program structure where the controlled machine is always in exactly one defined state with explicit named transitions based on conditions. States are exhaustive with every operating mode named and mutually exclusive with only one active per scan. Implementation: a DINT CurrentState tag plus a CASE/SELECT block. Benefits: predictable since exact mode is always known, debuggable by asking which transition condition is not satisfied, and auditable since complete behavior is visible in one CASE block. Common states: IDLE, READY, RUNNING, FAULTED, E_STOP, HOMING, PAUSED. State machines eliminate mystery-mode failures that cross-coupled ladder latches create. IEC 61131-3 SFC formalizes state machines graphically.'}, {'term': 'ISA-101', 'def': 'The ISA standard for High-Performance HMI graphics design. Core principle: normal and safe conditions are displayed in calm neutral grays and whites while color is reserved exclusively for abnormal states such as alarms and deviations. This maximizes alarm signal-to-noise: any color on a gray screen immediately signals that operator attention is needed. ISA-101 also specifies consistent process object standards for pump icons, valve shapes, and flow directions, along with alarm display hierarchy and level-of-detail drill-down from Overview to Group to Detail. Widely adopted after incident investigations showed that color-heavy rainbow HMIs desensitized operators to real alarms.'}, {'term': 'ISA-18.2', 'def': 'The ISA standard for industrial alarm management covering the full alarm lifecycle: rationalization deciding which conditions deserve alarms, documentation in a Master Alarm Database, implementation in PLC or DCS, performance monitoring, and management of change. Design targets: average alarm rate 1-2 per 10 minutes; peak rate 10 per 10 minutes; standing alarms 10 or fewer at any time. Each alarm must have a documented consequence, priority, and required operator response. ISA-18.2 was driven by incidents where alarm floods prevented operators from identifying root causes during process upsets. The standard mandates periodic alarm performance reviews and rationalization of nuisance alarms.'}, {'term': 'Alarm Flood', 'def': 'An overwhelming burst of alarms from a single process upset that cascades through inter-related equipment. The root-cause alarm is buried under hundreds of consequence alarms, forcing operators to silence the annunciator rather than respond intelligently. ISA-18.2 targets a manageable alarm rate of 1 to 2 alarms per 10 minutes per operator; a flood produces 10 to 100 or more per minute. Mitigation includes alarm suppression by state where downstream alarms are suppressed when a known upstream fault is active, cause-and-effect rationalization in a Master Alarm Database, shelving of nuisance alarms, and grouping related alarms into single event alarms.'}, {'term': 'Tag', 'def': 'A named memory address linking an HMI or SCADA display element to a live value inside a PLC or server. Every pump icon, trend chart, and numeric display binds to a tag. Tags carry a data type such as BOOL INT or REAL, a scan rate, and an engineering-unit label. In ControlLogix, tags replace fixed addresses like N7:0 so programs are self-documenting. A tag named Conveyor_1_SpeedFBK is immediately understood; N7:34 is not. SCADA and Historian systems manage thousands to millions of tags, each polled at a configured rate. Tag naming conventions directly affect long-term maintainability.'}, {'term': 'OT', 'def': 'Operational Technology — the hardware and software that monitors and controls physical processes: PLCs, DCS, safety controllers, SCADA, HMIs, drives, and industrial networks such as EtherNet/IP, PROFINET, and Modbus. OT prioritizes determinism, uptime, and safety over confidentiality, which is the opposite of IT priorities. OT systems have long lifecycles of 15 to 25 years, often run legacy operating systems, and use proprietary protocols not designed with network security in mind. IT/OT convergence creates cybersecurity risk as plant networks connect to corporate IT. IEC 62443 is the primary OT security standard defining zones, conduits, security levels, and supplier requirements.'}, {'term': 'Zones and Conduits', 'def': 'The IEC 62443 OT cybersecurity architecture for segmenting industrial networks. A Security Zone groups assets sharing the same security requirements and threat exposure: all safety PLCs in Zone 0 most critical, SCADA servers in Zone 1, engineer workstations in Zone 2. A Conduit is the controlled communication path between zones, implemented as a firewall, industrial DMZ, data diode, or ACL-controlled managed switch. Traffic between zones must pass through conduits where it is inspected, filtered, and logged. The more critical the zone, the fewer and tighter its conduits. Zones and conduits implement defense-in-depth, replacing the single-perimeter castle-and-moat model that fails when the perimeter is breached.'}, {'term': 'Edge Computing', 'def': 'Processing data at or near the machine on an IPC, gateway, or smart sensor rather than sending raw data to a central cloud server. Benefits: sub-millisecond response time, continued autonomous operation during network outages, reduced bandwidth by pre-processing and aggregating before upload, and local data sovereignty. Architecture split: edge handles time-critical control and local historian buffering; cloud handles long-term storage, cross-site analytics, ML training, and remote dashboards. Common edge platforms include Siemens SIMATIC IPC, Rockwell Stratix with compute module, Moxa AIG series gateways, and ruggedized Linux IPCs running Node-RED or Python pipelines.'}, {'term': 'Publish/Subscribe', 'def': 'A messaging pattern where publishers send messages to named topics on a broker without knowing who will receive them, and subscribers register interest in topics and receive matching messages without knowing the source. Decouples producers from consumers: new subscribers can join at any time without changing the publisher. MQTT implements pub/sub for IIoT. Contrast with request/response polling such as Modbus and OPC-UA where the client explicitly requests each value. Pub/sub scales well for broadcast data like sensor readings, alarms, and events. Request/response is better for on-demand reads of specific values. Both patterns coexist in modern IIoT architectures.'}, {'term': 'Broker', 'def': 'The central message router in MQTT architecture that receives all published messages and routes each to every active subscriber of that topic. Brokers such as Mosquitto, HiveMQ, and AWS IoT Core maintain subscriber lists, support QoS levels 0/1/2, and optionally retain the last value per topic for late-joining clients. Brokers support TLS encryption and certificate-based authentication. The broker decouples publishers from subscribers: a sensor publishes once regardless of how many consumers exist, and a new dashboard subscribes to existing topics without changing the sensor firmware at all.'}, {'term': 'TCP (robot)', 'def': 'Tool Center Point — the single reference point at the tip of the robot end-effector from which all programmed positions are calculated. Defined by a 6-DOF offset of X, Y, Z plus Rx, Ry, Rz from the robot wrist flange. A 1 mm TCP error propagates to every saved point. TCP calibration: teach the TCP against a fixed pin in four or more orientations; the controller solves for the offset that minimizes position scatter. A damaged or replaced tool requires TCP re-calibration before re-running programs. Most robot programs use Tool Frame with origin at the TCP or User Frame with origin at the workpiece, making programming intuitive and machine-relative.'}, {'term': 'Base Frame', 'def': 'The fixed World coordinate frame at the robot base mounting foot, the origin of the robot coordinate system. All TCP positions are expressed relative to this frame or a User Frame offset from it. When a robot is moved to a new base location, base-frame calibration must be performed so all saved programs still reference correct world positions. Contrast with Tool Frame centered on the TCP and User Frame aligned to the workpiece or fixture. A miscalibrated base frame shifts every programmed point by the same offset error.'}, {'term': 'Singularity', 'def': 'A robot configuration where two or more joint axes become coplanar or aligned, making inverse-kinematics math undefined or requiring near-infinite joint velocity to execute a finite TCP velocity. Types: wrist singularity where J4/J5/J6 axes align, shoulder singularity where TCP is directly over J1, and elbow singularity where the arm is fully extended or retracted. Near a singularity, computed joint speeds spike to extremes and the path becomes unpredictable. Mitigation: program paths through singular zones using joint moves where exact path does not matter, enable the controller singularity-avoidance mode, or re-orient the fixture to move the working zone away from singular poses.'}, {'term': 'Cobot', 'def': 'A collaborative robot designed per ISO/TS 15066 and ISO 10218 to operate safely near people without traditional hard guarding in specific operating modes. Uses power-and-force limiting, speed-and-separation monitoring, hand-guiding, and safety-rated monitored stop. Despite the design, a risk assessment per ISO 10218-2 is mandatory for every application — the cobot enables collaboration but the task may still require guarding based on tool sharpness, payload, and speed. Key platforms: Universal Robots UR series, FANUC CRX, ABB YuMi and GoFa, KUKA LBR iisy. Cobots dominate light assembly, inspection, and pick-and-place in flexible small-batch manufacturing.'}, {'term': 'Proportional (P)', 'def': 'The P term in PID multiplies present error by gain Kp to produce immediate corrective output: Output_P = Kp x Error. High Kp yields fast response but causes overshoot and oscillation. Low Kp is sluggish and slow to correct. P-only control always leaves a steady-state residual error because a nonzero output is needed to hold the process at setpoint, which requires nonzero error for P to produce it. P alone is adequate where a small offset is acceptable. P is always the first term tuned in any PID loop: set I and D to zero first, then increase Kp until the response is fast but not oscillating.'}, {'term': 'Integral (I)', 'def': 'The I term in PID sums accumulated error over time and adds it to controller output, eliminating steady-state offset. As long as any error exists, integral keeps growing and pushes output further. Ti controls the rate: small Ti means aggressive integration. Integral windup is the main failure mode: if the output saturates such as a valve fully open while error persists, integral accumulates to a large value and causes severe overshoot when the condition clears. Anti-windup circuits freeze or clamp the integral at saturation. I always pairs with P in PI control. Pure I-only control is impractical in industrial applications.'}, {'term': 'Derivative (D)', 'def': 'The D term in PID reacts to the rate of change of error, braking corrective action as error approaches zero to prevent overshoot: Output_D = Kd x d(error)/dt. High Kd smooths overshoot but massively amplifies measurement noise. Best practice: apply D to the process variable not the error setpoint, to avoid setpoint step changes creating large output spikes. Most industrial loops run PI only. D is added for processes with significant inertia such as large thermal masses or high-inertia servo axes where overshoot is costly. Always add derivative filtering when D is enabled to prevent noise amplification.'}, {'term': 'Open-Loop', 'def': 'A control system that commands an output based on setpoint alone with no measurement of the actual result. Examples include a toaster with a fixed timer, a V/Hz VFD with no speed feedback, a solenoid valve pulsed for a timed duration to dispense volume, and a stepper motor driven by step counts without position feedback. Open-loop is simpler and lower-cost but drifts with load changes, temperature, friction, and wear. Calibration must be repeated as conditions change. Used where closed-loop cost is not justified and accuracy requirements are relaxed, or where installing a feedback sensor is impractical.'}, {'term': 'Closed-Loop', 'def': 'A control system that continuously measures its output and uses feedback to self-correct toward a setpoint. Error = setpoint minus feedback; the controller adjusts output to drive error to zero. Examples: a VFD with encoder feedback maintaining 1750 RPM, a PID temperature controller reducing heat as the oven approaches setpoint, a servo using encoder position to achieve 0.01 mm accuracy. Closed-loop systems self-correct for disturbances such as load changes, friction, and wear, but can oscillate if poorly tuned. Contrast with Open-Loop, which commands based on setpoint alone and cannot self-correct.'}, {'term': 'Hierarchy of Controls', 'def': 'NIOSH/OSHA risk-reduction hierarchy ranked most to least effective: 1. Elimination, design the hazard out entirely. 2. Substitution, replace with a safer alternative. 3. Engineering controls such as guards, interlocks, light curtains, and ventilation. 4. Administrative controls including procedures, training, permits, and warning labels. 5. PPE, the last resort that relies on human compliance. Higher levels are more reliable because they do not depend on human behavior. IEC 62061 and ISO 13849 embed this hierarchy: engineering safeguards must be implemented before administrative controls are credited in a safety function design.'}, {'term': 'Voltage Drop Method', 'def': 'A live troubleshooting technique that measures voltage drop across a connection under full load current to detect abnormal resistance. Good connections drop less than 100 mV; a corroded terminal or worn contact may drop 1 to 5 V. Unlike a resistance measurement that requires de-energization, the voltage-drop method works live and reveals resistance that only manifests under load heating. Apply meter leads across the suspect element such as contactor contacts, terminal blocks, bus bars, fuses, or interlock switch contacts. Any reading exceeding manufacturer spec warrants cleaning or replacement. Also called the millivolt-drop test.'}, {'term': 'Live-Dead-Live', 'def': 'The NFPA 70E-required verification sequence before working on a de-energized circuit. Step 1 Live: test your meter on a known-live source to confirm it works and reads correctly. Step 2 Dead: test the target circuit to confirm 0 V de-energized. Step 3 Live again: retest on the known-live source to confirm the meter still works after the dead test. Skipping Step 3 allows an undetected meter failure after Step 1 to give a false-zero reading in Step 2, exposing the technician to full voltage. Always complete all three steps every time. NFPA 70E Section 120.5 requires this as part of establishing an electrically safe work condition.'}, {'term': 'Availability', 'def': 'Availability A = MTBF divided by (MTBF + MTTR), the fraction of scheduled time a system is operational. A pump with MTBF=2000 h and MTTR=8 h has A=2000/2008=99.6%. Availability captures both failure frequency via MTBF and repair speed via MTTR. Improving MTBF requires reliability programs and PdM. Improving MTTR requires spare parts availability, documented repair procedures, and trained technicians. World-class manufacturing targets above 95% availability for critical equipment. OEE equals Availability times Performance times Quality, making availability one of three OEE dimensions.'}, {'term': 'Vibration Signature', 'def': 'The characteristic frequency pattern of a specific mechanical fault in rotating equipment. Unbalance shows a dominant 1x running speed peak. Misalignment produces 1x and 2x peaks with elevated axial component. Mechanical looseness generates a forest of harmonics at 0.5x, 1x, 2x, 3x and higher. Bearing outer-race defect appears at BPFO frequency; inner-race at BPFI; rolling element at BSF; cage at FTF — all calculated from bearing geometry. A healthy machine shows a clean 1x peak and low broadband noise floor. Trending characteristic-frequency amplitudes over weeks allows planned replacement before catastrophic failure, which is the foundation of PdM programs for rotating equipment.'}, {'term': 'RCM', 'def': 'Reliability-Centered Maintenance, a methodology per SAE JA1011 and JA1012 for selecting the most cost-effective maintenance strategy per asset based on function, failure modes, failure effects, and criticality. RCM asks seven questions: What does the asset do? What is a functional failure? What causes each failure? What is the effect? What is the consequence? Is a proactive task technically feasible and worth doing? If not, what else? Output is a strategy matrix: run-to-failure for low-criticality cheap-to-replace assets, time-based PM for predictable wear, condition-based PdM for detectable degradation, or redesign for unacceptable failure consequences. RCM prevents both over-maintaining and under-maintaining assets.'}, {'term': 'Commissioning', 'def': 'The structured bottom-up process of verifying a control system before production. Five phases: (1) Pre-power: point-to-point wire check, torque terminals, megger motor cables. (2) I/O checkout: force each channel and confirm field device response. (3) Functional test: verify each sequence in isolation including motor starts, stops, interlocks, and safety circuits. (4) Integrated test: run all sequences together under load. (5) Performance acceptance: confirm throughput, speed, and tolerance specs are met. Commissioning generates punch-list documentation, as-built updates, and test records forming the maintenance baseline. Skipping Phase 1 or 2 typically causes wiring faults that damage equipment on first energization.'}, {'term': 'R_TRIG / F_TRIG', 'def': 'IEC 61131-3 standard function blocks for edge detection in Structured Text and FBD. R_TRIG output Q is TRUE for one scan when CLK transitions from FALSE to TRUE. F_TRIG output Q is TRUE for one scan when CLK goes from TRUE to FALSE. Must be instantiated in a VAR block: myEdge: R_TRIG; then called each scan: myEdge(CLK:=StartButton); read myEdge.Q as a one-shot TRUE. The instance stores the previous CLK state to detect transitions. This is why R_TRIG must be a VAR instance and not a temporary function call — the internal state is essential. R_TRIG/F_TRIG are the IEC 61131-3 portable replacement for the ladder OSR/OSF one-shot instructions.'}, {'term': 'One-shot (ONS/OSR/OSF)', 'def': 'An instruction producing TRUE output for exactly one scan on a signal transition. OSR fires once on the FALSE-to-TRUE transition; OSF fires once on TRUE-to-FALSE. The instruction uses a storage bit to remember previous state. Essential uses: increment a counter once per button press not every scan while held, trigger a single event on the first scan after E-stop clears, and latch an alarm on first occurrence. Without one-shots, any action inside a continuously-true rung executes every scan at 1 to 10 ms intervals, rapidly incrementing counters or overwriting setpoints. Always verify the OSR storage bit is not shared between multiple OSR instructions, which causes incorrect edge detection.'}, {'term': 'Double-coil', 'def': 'A PLC programming defect where the same output bit appears on more than one OTE rung or ST assignment. Because outputs write once per scan in rung order, only the last-executed write determines the final state. Example: Rung 10 sets Motor_Run=TRUE; Rung 45 unconditionally sets Motor_Run=FALSE — Motor_Run is always FALSE. Studio 5000 generates a duplicate-output warning but allows download. Detection: right-click the tag, choose Cross Reference, list all OTE/OTL/OTU/MOV write locations. Fix: consolidate all enabling conditions into one output rung, or replace with intentional OTL/OTU set-reset pairs where latching is truly needed. Double-coil is one of the top programming defects found in PLC code audits.'}, {'term': 'IN_OUT parameter', 'def': 'An AOI or IEC function block parameter passed by reference where the caller actual tag address is used directly inside the block rather than copying a value. Changes take effect in caller data immediately. Contrast: INPUT parameters are copied into the block read-only; OUTPUT parameters are copied back to the caller after execution. IN_OUT is used for large UDTs that would be expensive to copy, or for parameters the block must both read and modify in the same call. Passing a constant, expression, or literal as IN_OUT causes a compile error — only a real tag reference is legal. IN_OUT parameters appear in Studio 5000 as pass-by-reference and are flagged as modifiable in the instruction description.'}, {'term': 'Hysteresis (deadband)', 'def': 'Two separated switching thresholds that prevent rapid on/off cycling when a measured value fluctuates near a setpoint. Output turns ON above the upper threshold and turns OFF only below the lower threshold; between thresholds it holds its last state. Example: a cooling fan turns ON at 85 degrees C and OFF at 70 degrees C — steady thermal fluctuations around 78 degrees C do not cause cycling. Without hysteresis, a single threshold causes the output to chatter at rates limited only by controller scan time, damaging relay contacts and confusing operators. In relay logic, hysteresis is implemented with an upper-threshold XIC and lower-threshold XIO in parallel.'}, {'term': 'Watchdog timer', 'def': 'A hardware supervisory timer inside the CPU that must be reset each scan cycle by the scan executive. If a scan exceeds the watchdog timeout, the CPU trips a major fault and de-energizes all outputs to their safe state. Protects against unbounded FOR/WHILE loops in Structured Text that hang the CPU, deadlocked task scheduling, and software corruption. Never write WHILE TRUE or unbounded loops in PLC ST — always include an explicit exit condition or maximum iteration count. In ControlLogix, a Major Fault F16#04 identifies the watchdog-tripping task. Set per-task watchdog conservatively at 5 to 10 times the normal scan time. The watchdog is why PLC languages restrict or warn on unbounded loops.'}, {'term': 'Seal-in (latch)', 'def': 'A three-wire motor control rung where the output coil own auxiliary normally-open contact is wired in parallel with the momentary Start pushbutton. When Start is pressed, the coil energizes; the aux contact closes and bypasses Start, keeping the coil energized after Start is released. The circuit stays sealed in until the series Stop NC button is pressed, which breaks the circuit, de-energizes the coil, and opens the aux contact simultaneously. In PLC ladder, an XIC of the output bit in parallel with the Start XIC achieves the same effect. Seal-in is the foundation of all motor start-stop control. Without it, releasing Start immediately stops the motor with no maintained run condition.'}, {'term': 'Time literal (T#)', 'def': 'The IEC 61131-3 Structured Text syntax for expressing a duration constant: T# followed by hours h, minutes m, seconds s, and/or milliseconds ms. Examples: T#500ms, T#3s, T#1m30s, T#2h. Used to assign preset time to a timer: myTON(IN:=Enable, PT:=T#5s). In Studio 5000, timer presets store as DINT milliseconds so T#5s equals 5000. T# literals make code self-documenting: 5000 could be 5 seconds, 5000 milliseconds, or an arbitrary count; T#5s is unambiguous. Always use T# literals in production code instead of raw integers. In IEC-compliant runtimes, assigning an INT to a TIME-typed variable causes a type mismatch compile error.'}, {'term': 'Ampacity', 'def': 'The maximum continuous current a conductor can carry without exceeding its insulation temperature rating. Set by NEC tables (60/75/90 C columns) and derated for high ambient temperature and for bundling many conductors in one raceway.'}, {'term': 'Arc flash', 'def': 'An explosive release of energy from an electrical fault - heat up to 35,000 F, pressure blast, and molten metal. NFPA 70E defines the approach boundaries and required PPE. A separate hazard from shock.'}, {'term': 'kVA (apparent power)', 'def': 'Kilovolt-amperes - the total power the utility must supply, the vector sum of real (kW) and reactive (kVAR) power. Transformers and generators are rated in kVA because they must carry the full current regardless of power factor.'}, {'term': 'kVAR (reactive power)', 'def': 'Kilovolt-amperes reactive - power that oscillates back and forth to build the magnetic fields in motors and transformers. It does no net work but still requires current, so it must be supplied or corrected with capacitors.'}, {'term': 'Wye (star)', 'def': 'A three-phase connection where the three windings share a common neutral point. Provides two usable voltages: line-to-line (e.g. 480 V) and line-to-neutral (e.g. 277 V), related by the factor sqrt(3) = 1.732.'}, {'term': 'Delta', 'def': 'A three-phase connection where the windings form a closed triangle with no neutral. Line voltage equals phase voltage; line current = phase current x sqrt(3). Common on motor windings and delta-supplied services.'}, {'term': 'Line vs phase voltage', 'def': 'In a three-phase wye system, line voltage V_L is measured between two line conductors such as 480 V and phase voltage V_ph is line-to-neutral such as 277 V. Relation: V_L = V_ph x 1.732. In delta, no neutral exists so line voltage equals phase voltage. Dual-voltage motors such as 460Y/230 delta reconnect internally: wye for 460 V, delta for 230 V service — the voltage per winding is identical in both configurations. Always verify the motor nameplate connection method against supply voltage before energizing. Wye-delta reduced-voltage starters connect the motor in wye during starting then switch to delta at full speed, reducing starting inrush to one-third of across-the-line.'}, {'term': 'Contactor', 'def': 'An electrically operated switch (a heavy-duty relay) whose coil pulls in power contacts to start/stop a motor or load. Rated by NEMA size or IEC amperage; combined with an overload relay it becomes a motor starter.'}, {'term': 'Overload relay', 'def': 'Protects a motor from sustained over-current (not short circuit). Thermal or electronic, selected by trip class (10/20/30 = seconds to trip at 600% FLA). Its normally-closed contact opens the control circuit to drop the contactor.'}, {'term': 'Control transformer (CPT)', 'def': 'A small transformer that steps line voltage (e.g. 480 V) down to a safe control voltage (typically 120 V or 24 V) for the pilot devices and coils in a control circuit. Fused per NEC 450 on the primary and secondary.'}, {'term': 'Two-wire control', 'def': 'A maintained-contact control scheme (e.g. a float or pressure switch directly on the coil) that AUTO-RESTARTS when power returns after an outage - a hazard for personnel, so it is used only where restart is safe.'}, {'term': 'Equipment grounding conductor (EGC)', 'def': 'The green/bare conductor bonding non-current-carrying metal (enclosures, conduit, frames) back to the source. It carries fault current on a low-impedance path so the breaker trips fast; normally carries zero current.'}, {'term': 'GFCI', 'def': 'Ground-fault circuit interrupter - compares current leaving on the hot with current returning on the neutral and trips (typically at ~5 mA within 25 ms) if the difference means current is leaking through a person or fault to ground.'}, {'term': 'Full-load amps (FLA)', 'def': 'The current a motor draws at rated voltage, frequency, and full mechanical load, per its nameplate. Used to size conductors, overloads, and drives. Starting (locked-rotor) current is typically 6-8x FLA.'}, {'term': 'Inrush current', 'def': 'The brief high-amplitude current surge when a motor starts or transformer is first energized. Motor inrush: 6 to 8 times full-load amps lasting 2 to 10 cycles while the rotor accelerates and back-EMF builds. Transformer magnetizing inrush: 8 to 15 times rated current for a few milliseconds due to core saturation on the first half-cycle. NEC motor-circuit overcurrent devices are sized to ride through inrush: inverse-time breakers up to 250 percent FLA, dual-element time-delay fuses up to 175 percent FLA. Soft starters limit inrush by ramping voltage over 2 to 30 seconds. VFDs limit inrush by ramping frequency from near-zero, protecting contactors, transformers, and supply buses.'}, {'term': 'HOA switch', 'def': 'Hand-Off-Auto selector switch — the three-position operator override for PLC output control. HAND energizes the load directly from the switch, bypassing PLC logic and all software interlocks. OFF de-energizes the load regardless of PLC state. AUTO routes load control through the PLC output normally. HOA wiring typically bypasses the PLC output module for HAND so a failed PLC cannot prevent manual operation. Critical safety requirement: in HAND mode all PLC safety interlocks are bypassed — HAND must be prohibited during LOTO work and guarded equipment access. Always indicate HOA position with a pilot light and document required position in permits to work.'}]
